"PD Completed Project – HOHUPGROUP – BUILDING EXCELLENT TOGETHER (https://www.hohupgroup.com.my/property-development/pdcompleted/)\nciteturn266view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.hohupgroup.com.my/property-development/pdcompleted/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 489\nL0:   * 603-5033 2788\nL1:   * corporate@hohupgroup.com.my\nL2: \nL3:   * cite0†Home L4:   * cite1†About Us L5:     * cite2†Corporate Information L6:     * cite3†Corporate Structure L7:   * cite4†Investor Relations L8:     * cite5†Annual Reports & AGM & EGM L9:     * cite6†Bursa Updates L10:   * cite4†Division L11:     * cite7†Construction L12:       * cite8†Completed Project L13:     * cite9†Property Development L14:       * cite10†Current Project L15:       * cite11†Completed Project L16:   * cite12†Career L17:   * cite13†Contact Us L18: \nL19: [Input: Enter your search] [Input]\nL20: # Completed Project\nL21: \nL22: cite9†About Us L23: \nL24: cite14†Current Projects L25: \nL26: cite15†Completed Projects L27: \nL28: cite16†Image L29: \nL30: ## AURORA PLACE\nL31: @BUKIT JALIL\nL32: \nL33: cite17†Image L34: \nL35: ## AURORA SOVO\nL36: @BUKIT JALIL\nL37: \nL38: cite18†Image L39: \nL40: ## JALIL SUTERA SEMI-D P7B\nL41: @BUKIT JALIL\nL42: \nL43: cite19†Image L44: \nL45: ## JALIL SUTERA SEMI-D P7A\nL46: @BUKIT JALIL\nL47: \nL48: cite20†Image L49: \nL50: ## JALIL SUTERA SUPERLINK\nL51: @BUKIT JALIL\nL52: \nL53: cite21†Image L54: \nL55: ## JALIL DAMAI\nL56: @BUKIT JALIL\nL57: \nL58: cite22†Image L59: \nL60: ## THE RITZ\nL61: @BUKIT JALIL\nL62: \nL63: cite23†Image L64: ## SRI RAKYAT\nL65: @BUKIT JALIL\nL66: \nL67: Copyright 2024 © Ho Hup Construction Company Berhad cite24†Image L68: \nL69: [Input][Input]\nL70: \nL71: Mr. Haikel Bin Ismail\nL72: Mr. Haikel Bin Ismail is a seasoned Board advisor and senior executive with over 25 years of leadership experience in strategy, finance, governance, risk, and compliance. He has extensive experience advising Boards and Board Committees on enterprise risk, internal control, major investments, and complex governance matters across public-listed and government-linked organisations.\nL73: Mr. Haikel Bin Ismail has held Group-level leadership roles including Chief Risk Officer and Chief Internal Auditor, where he led enterprise-wide governance, risk, and assurance frameworks and oversaw large-scale capital projects, mergers and acquisitions, and transformation initiatives. His background spans highly regulated and capital-intensive sectors including telecommunications, oil and gas, utilities, transportation, property, and construction.\nL74: Mr. Haikel Bin Ismail currently serving as Independent Non-Executive Director and Audit Committee Chairman of Omesti Berhad, while undertaking advisory work through AEQUITY Sdn Bhd and related assignments focused on due diligence, acquisition risk, financial sustainability, governance and transformation.\nL75: Mr. Haikel Bin Ismail brings strong independence of thought and sound judgment, supported by deep expertise in risk oversight, financial stewardship, internal audit, and fraud investigations. He is known for his ability to challenge constructively, provide clear insight to Boards, and strengthen organisational resilience, accountability, and long-term value creation.\nL76: \nL77: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL78: \nL79: [Input][Input]\nL80: \nL81: Ms. Tan Guan Ling\nL82: Ms Tan Guan Ling is a senior professional with over 25 years of experience across construction, property development, investment holding, and manufacturing sectors.\nL83: \nL84: Ms Tan Guan Ling began her career as a Finance Executive at MBF Holdings Berhad in 1996. Prior to joining the company on 23 September 2019 as Senior Manager, she held various positions in corporate finance and accounting functions with several companies, primarily public-listed entities.\nL85: Ms Tan Guan Ling was subsequently promoted to Assistant General Manager, where she heads the Corporate and Finance Department. In this role, she oversees corporate finance, treasury, budgeting, statutory reporting, Bursa compliance, and board reporting functions.\nL86: \nL87: \nL88: \nL89: Ms Tan Guan Ling is a Chartered Accountant and a member of the Malaysian Institute of Accountants (MIA) as well as an Associate member of the Chartered Institute of Management Accountants (CIMA).\nL90: \nL91: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL92: [Input][Input]\nL93: \nL94: Mr. Bernard Chen Tong Liang\nL95: \nL96: Mr. Bernard Chen Tong Liang has over three decades of experience in corporate finance, banking, asset management, and business advisory. Currently serving as Executive Director at ThinkBridge Advisory Sdn Bhd since 2017, he leads corporate consultancy initiatives focused on debt restructuring, business turnaround strategies, and sustainable growth models for both public and private companies.\nL97: Previously, he was the Chief Executive Officer of BIB Insurance Brokers Sdn Bhd, a Hong Leong Group company, where he was responsible for driving business expansion and improving operational efficiency. Prior to that, he held senior roles at OCBC Bank (Malaysia) Bhd and its subsidiary, Horizon Asset Management Sdn Bhd, where he managed distressed and special situation assets, and led the creation of a specialised vehicle to invest in non-performing loans and distressed property assets.\nL98: Earlier roles include heading the Group Special Assets Division at Alliance Bank Malaysia Berhad, managing major non-performing and strategic assets, and serving as Senior Manager, Recover Operations at Pengurusan Danaharta Nasional Berhad, Malaysia's national asset management company, where he led complex corporate debt restructuring exercises. His career also includes investment banking experience with Perdana Merchant Bankers Berhad and early roles in credit, project analysis, and corporate finance.\nL99: His expertise spans debt recovery, asset restructuring, risk management, and financial advisory across diverse sectors and geographies.\nL100: \nL101: \nL102: Mr. Bernard Chen Tong Liang does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL103: \nL104: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL105: \nL106: [Input][Input]\nL107: \nL108: Mr. Narayanasamy A/L N Chithambaram\nL109: \nL110: Mr. Narayanasamy A/L N Chithambaram is a legal professional with a career spanning over three decades. Graduating with a B.A. (Hon) in Law, Sociology, and Social Anthropology from the University of Keele, United Kingdom, in 1990, he brings a wealth of expertise in corporate law and Information Technology Law.\nL111: His career journey includes essential roles in various organisations, such as serving as the Group Legal Advisor -General Counsel at Haisan Resources Berhad, General Counsel at YFG Berhad, Legal Advisor at Lityan Holdings Berhad, Chief Operating Officer at Bheesma Logistics (M) Sdn Bhd, Head of Corporate Division/Assistant of Managing Director of Pembinaan Nadzri Sdn Bhd and Law Lecturer at Stamford College.\nL112: A results-driven professional, he boasts extensive experience in corporate transactions, including takeover bids, mergers and acquisitions (M&A), joint ventures, and restructuring. With a focus on legal, regulatory, and compliance matters, he is also proficient in intellectual property and various legal domains. From Corporate Law to Information Technology Law, he excels in drafting contracts for significant commercial transactions, arbitration management, and overseeing liquidation.\nL113: Additionally, his expertise extends to conducting comprehensive legal due diligence for M&A, encompassing thorough research into target companies histories, intellectual property, and share data.\nL114: Mr. Narayanasamy A/L N Chithambaram currently sits on the Board of Microlink Solutions Berhad as Independent Non-Executive Director.\nL115: \nL116: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL117: \nL118: [Input][Input]\nL119: \nL120: Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong\nL121: Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong was appointed to the Board of the Company as an Executive Director on 12 August 2010, to manage the financial and corporate restructuring of the Group. On 24 April 2014, Dato’ Wong was re-designated as the Executive Director/Chief Executive Officer (“CEO”) of the Company. Subsequently, Dato’ Wong was re-designated to Executive Director on 21 March 2024.\nL122: Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong began his career with Citibank Malaysia in 1995 before moving to Abric Berhad in 1999. During his tenure in Abric Berhad up to 2007, he served as Executive Director/Chief Operating Officer.\nL123: \nL124: \nL125: \nL126: Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL127: \nL128: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL129: ### Tan Sri Datuk Seri (DR) Mazlan Bin Lazim\nL130: Tan Sri Datuk Seri (DR) Mazlan Bin Lazim has held a long and distinguished career with Polis Di Raja Malaysia (PDRM – Royal Malaysian Police), rising through the ranks. Now retired from PDRM, he most recently served as the Deputy Inspector-General of Police of Malaysia (DIG) from June to December 2021 under Inspector-General (IGP) Acryl Sani Abdullah Sani.\nL131: He also held the position of Director of the Bukit Aman Logistics & Technology Department at PDRM and had stints as police chief for Kelantan and Kuala Lumpur.\nL132: Tan Sri Datuk Seri (DR) Mazlan Bin Lazim currently sits on the Board of Computer Forms (Malaysia) Berhad, AHB Holdings Berhad, and Microlink Solution Berhad as Independent Non-Executive Director.\nL133: \nL134: [Button: × ]\nL135: ### Mr. Thevanaigam Rajan Chitty\nL136: Mr. Thevanaigam Rajan Chitty, a Malaysian aged 65, was appointed to the Board of the Company as an Independent Non-Executive Director, Audit Committee Chairman and Nomination Committee on 23 March 2022.\nL137: Mr. Rajan is a a Member of Malaysian Institute of Accountants (MIA) and Associate Member of the Chartered Institute of Management Accountants (CIMA) UK. He is a finance and corporate strategy professional with extensive experience in all aspects of corporate finance at senior level across various industry sectors, particularly plantations and automotive.\nL138: His career began in 1980 with Golden Hope Plantations Berhad (formerly Harrisons & Crosfield) where he progressed to become Group Financial Accountant. In 1992, he joined G Team Consultants Sdn Bhd as Manager, Corporate Affairs, and was involved in a reverse takeover exercise.\nL139: In 1994, Mr Rajan joined Perusahaan Otomobil Kedua Sdn Bhd – Perodua – initially as General Manager, Corporate Planning & Finance, handling the responsibilities of Group CFO. He was closely involved in the restructure of Perodua with the Japanese partners gaining 51 % equity and control in Perodua’s manufacturing arm. On completion of the exercise in December 2001, he was appointed to the Board of the newly established JV companies and designated as Executive Director.\nL140: In 2008, Mr Rajan joined Johnson Controls Automotive Holdings/ Adient Malaysia as General Manager/ Managing Director, working to revive the company’s performance and achieving a return to profitability through operational improvements and efficiencies. Between 2012 and 2021, he took on several roles in the India Southeast Asia and Asia Pacific regions, focusing on the commercial and strategic planning aspects of the business to expand and strengthen the footprint of the organisation\nL141: He does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL142: \nL143: [Button: × ]\nL144: ### Mr. Danny Hoe Kam Thong\nL145: \nL146: Danny Hoe Kam Thong began his career with Ernst & Young, where he worked for a period of 14 years in a number of positions, including a secondment to the firms New York office, as well as rising to Principal for Audit & Advisory Services.\nL147: In 2001, Mr. Danny joined Kellogg Asia Sdn Bhd as Financial & Reporting Manager, and was closely involved in the merger of Kellogg South East Asia and its Greater China business. A year later, he began a long association with the Interflour Group, one of the largest flour milling conglomerates in the Asia Pacific region. As Group Chief Financial Officer, he contributed to the turnaround of the business which saw substantial growth in volume and market share.\nL148: In August 2011, he relocated to Vietnam as Chief Executive Officer and Country Head of Interflour Vietnam Ltd, taking the flour business to record performance in 2013 and 2014. From 2015 to 2018, still based in Vietnam, he was appointed as Executive Director – Asean at Pilmico Foods Corporation, Philippines, with the remit to transform the traditional family business into a regional organisation.\nL149: After a short stint back in Malaysia with Tradewinds Plantations Berhad as lead senior consultant on a national grain purchasing, storage and management project for the Government of Malaysia, he rejoined the Interflour Group, this time as Chief Executive Officer & Country Head of its Turkey operations.\nL150: \nL151: Mr. Danny is also Independent Non-Executive Director for Kumpulan FIMA Berhad and Omesti Berhad.\nL152: \nL153: [Button: × ]\nL154: \nL155: cite25†Image: Flex Sovo - 2K L156: ## Flex Sovo, Bukit Jalil\nL157: \nL158: ##### Exquisitely crafted with indelible class. A prestigious development with leading modern design and contemporary architecture. Strategically located in the Southern Gateway of Kuala Lumpur, Bukit Jalil City with excellent connectivity via major highway networks and public amenities such as Pavilion Bukit Jalil Megamall, Bukit Jalil Park, and National Sport Centre will be the epicentre of new vibrant Suburban Township.\nL159: ##### From 376 – 591 sqft\nL160: From RM 404,200 onwards\nL161: \nL162: cite26†WEBSITE†flexsovo.com.my L163: \nL164: [Button: × ]\nL165: \nL166: cite27†Image: Golden Sails 2 - 2K L167: \nL168: ## The Crown, Kota Kinabalu\nL169: ##### Standing tall next to the best neighbours, The Crown is on the last parcel of land in the most desirable part of Kota Kinabalu, with a host of amenities like entertainment outlets, shops and hospitals. Its finest neighbour is the Crowne Plaza Hotel-a five-star hotel with a sterling reputation throughout the world and a welcome addition to the Kota Kinabalu hospitality scene.\nL170: ##### From 394 – 4,852 sq ft\nL171: From RM499,000 onwards.\nL172: \nL173: cite28†WEBSITE†thecrownkk.com L174: \nL175: cite29†GALLERY†thecrownkk.com L176: \nL177: cite30†Contact Us†thecrownkk.com L178: \nL179: [Button: × ]\nL180: \nL181: cite31†Image: Nadira Residensi 01 - 2K L182: \nL183: ## Laman Iskandaria, Kulai\nL184: ###### A 429-acre development, Laman Iskandaria is a master planned community comprising of landed residences and commercial shoplots. The contemporary and innovative design feature incorporating natural surrounding amidst the hustle and bustle of the city.\nL185: First phase launch of the 99.83 acres of Laman Iskandaria, Nadira Residensi, 2 storey terrace house, targeted at the affordable housing segment to meet the growing demand for this housing in the vicinity and The SouQ, 2 storey shoplots will be the commercial units to cater the residents. This promising development brings you an ultimate experience in serenity, joy and harmonious lifestyle with plentiful opportunities for all.\nL186: ###### Phase 1A & 1B\nL187: From 1,429 – 1,569 sqft\nL188: From RM332,000 onwards.\nL189: \nL190: cite32†WEBSITE†lamaniskandaria.com.my L191: \nL192: cite33†GALLERY†lamaniskandaria.com.my L193: \nL194: cite34†Contact Us†lamaniskandaria.com.my L195: \nL196: [Button: × ]\nL197: ### Mr. Low Kheng Lun\nL198: \nL199: Mr. Low Kheng Lun, a Malaysian, aged 35, was appointed to the Board of the Company as a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director on 31 October 2011.\nL200: \nL201: He graduated from European Business School, London, United Kingdom (“UK”) with MA (Hons) Entrepreneurial Management and University College London, UK with BEng (Hons) Civil Engineering.\nL202: He served as a Graduate Engineer in Buildings and Infrastructure Department in Scott Wilson PLC, London, UK from 2008 to 2009. He was an IT Advisory Associate in KPMG. He is currently a Director of Catalyst Interior Design Sdn. Bhd. and a Director of Low Chee Group Sdn. Bhd.\nL203: \nL204: He is the son of Dato’ Low Tuck Choy, a major shareholder of the Company and Datin Chan Bee Leng, a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director of the Company.\nL205: \nL206: He does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL207: \nL208: [Button: × ]\nL209: ### Datin Chan Bee Leng\nL210: \nL211: Datin Chan Bee Leng, a Malaysian, aged 61, was appointed to the Board of the Company as a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director on 31 October 2011.\nL212: \nL213: Datin Chan graduated from University of Warwick, United Kingdom (“UK”) with a BSc (Hons) Management Science (Majoring in Finance & Marketing) and from Weston-Super-Mare Technical College, UK with GCSE ‘A’ Levels & Business Education Council Diploma (BEC).\nL214: Datin Chan was a Finance Manager in Brisdale Sdn. Bhd. (a subsidiary of developer Perwira Indra Sakti Sdn. Bhd.) from 1990 to 1996. She was a Director of Tepat Concrete Sdn. Bhd. from 2000 to 2006. She subsequently joined Bukit Jalil Development Sdn. Bhd. as a Consultant from 2006 to 2008.\nL215: \nL216: Datin Chan is the spouse of Dato’ Low Tuck Choy, a major shareholder of the Company and the mother to Mr. Low Kheng Lun, a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director of the Company.\nL217: Datin Chan does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL218: \nL219: [Button: × ]\nL220: ### Dato’ Sri Thong Kok Khee\nL221: \nL222: Dato’ Sri Thong Kok Khee, a Malaysian, aged 67, was appointed to the Board of the Company as a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director on 6 October 2011. He is a member of the Remuneration Committee and subsequently was appointed as the Chairman of the Remuneration Committee on 25 August 2014.\nL223: A graduate from the London School of Economics, United Kingdom, Dato’ Sri Thong has worked in the financial services industry from 1979 to 1988. He worked for Standard Chartered Merchant Bank Asia Limited in Singapore from October 1982 to June 1988 and his last held position was Director of its Corporate Finance Division. Dato’ Sri Thong is the Executive Deputy Chairman cum Chief Executive Officer of Insas Berhad.\nL224: He is also a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director of Inari Amertron Berhad and Omesti Berhad.\nL225: Dato’ Sri Thong is a major shareholder of the Company.\nL226: \nL227: [Button: × ]\nL228: ### Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong\nL229: \nL230: Dato’ Wong Kit-Leong, a Malaysian, aged 50, was appointed to the Board of the Company as an Executive Director on 12 August 2010, to manage the financial and corporate restructuring of the Group. He was re-designated as Chief Executive Officer (CEO) of the Company by the Board on 24 April 2014.\nL231: \nL232: Dato’ Wong graduated with a Bachelor of Commerce from the University of British Columbia, Canada in 1995.\nL233: He began his career with Citibank Malaysia in 1995 before moving to Abric Berhad in 1999. During his tenure in Abric up to 2007, he served as Executive Director/Chief Operating Officer.\nL234: \nL235: Dato’ Wong is a Shareholder and Director in Red Zone Development Sdn. Bhd., a major shareholder of the Company by virtue of its substantial shareholding in Omesti Berhad which is the holding company of Omesti Holdings Berhad.\nL236: \nL237: Dato’ Wong does not hold any directorship in other public companies.\nL238: \nL239: [Button: × ]\nL240: ### Dato’ Wong Gian Kui\nL241: \nL242: Dato’ Wong Gian Kui, a Malaysian aged 60, was appointed to the Board of the Company as an Executive Director on 21st August 2017\nL243: Dato’ Wong is an accountant by profession and was articled with Harun Oh and Wong, Chartered Accountants and the Malaysian member firm of Horwath International in 1981. He qualified as CPA in 1984 and was admitted as a member of MICPA in 1985 and as member of MIA in 1988. Dato’ Wong held a senior management position in Harun, Oh & Wong and was seconded to Stoy Hayward London, Chartered Accountants and the UK member firm of Horwath International for two (2) years from 1990 to 1991.\nL244: He is also the Chief Executive Officer (CEO) of Insas, Executive Director of Inari Amertron Berhad, and a Non-Independent Non-Executive Director of SYF Resources Berhad.\nL245: \nL246: [Button: × ]\nL247: ### Aurora Place\nL248: \nL249: #### @ Bukit Jalil\nL250: \nL251: ###### Comprising of 3, 4, and 5-storey shop-offices, this modern up-market hybrid mall is set to blaze Bukit Jalil landscape as the “Happening Place” upon its expected completion in September 2016.\nL252: \nL253: cite35†GALLERY L254: \nL255: [Button: × ]\nL256: \nL257: ## cite36†Image L258: \nL259: ### Deep Foundation Works\nL260: \nL261: [popuppress id=”1979″]\nL262: \nL263: [Button: × ]\nL264: \nL265: ## cite37†Image L266: \nL267: ### Oil Gas Works\nL268: \nL269: [popuppress id=”1977″]\nL270: \nL271: [Button: × ]\nL272: \nL273: ## cite38†Image L274: \nL275: ### Office Buildings\nL276: \nL277: [popuppress id=”1975″]\nL278: \nL279: [Button: × ]\nL280: \nL281: ## cite39†Image L282: ### Stadium\nL283: \nL284: [popuppress id=”1973″]\nL285: \nL286: [Button: × ]\nL287: \nL288: ## cite40†Image L289: \nL290: ### Airport Terminal\nL291: \nL292: [popuppress id=”1971″]\nL293: \nL294: [Button: × ]\nL295: \nL296: ## cite41†Image L297: \nL298: ### Offshore Marine Structures\nL299: \nL300: [popuppress id=”1969″]\nL301: \nL302: [Button: × ]\nL303: \nL304: ## cite42†Image L305: \nL306: ### Water Supply Schemes / Dams / Sewage Treatments\nL307: \nL308: [popuppress id=”1967″]\nL309: \nL310: [Button: × ]\nL311: \nL312: ## cite43†Image L313: \nL314: ### Railways & Mass Rapid Transit\nL315: \nL316: [popuppress id=”1954″]\nL317: \nL318: [Button: × ]\nL319: \nL320: ## cite44†Image L321: \nL322: ### Highways & Bridges\nL323: \nL324: [popuppress id=”1963″]\nL325: \nL326: [Button: × ]\nL327: \nL328: ## cite45†Image L329: ### Sri Rakyat Apartment\nL330: \nL331: ###### Total Units: 1,098 units\nL332: Completed Year: 2003\nL333: Sales Status: Sold out\nL334: \nL335: [Button: × ]\nL336: \nL337: ## cite46†Image L338: \nL339: ### Jalil Damai Apartment\nL340: \nL341: ###### Total Units: 930 units\nL342: Completed Year: 2006\nL343: Sales Status: Sold out\nL344: \nL345: [Button: × ]\nL346: \nL347: ## cite47†Image L348: \nL349: ### Jalil Sutera – SuperLink House\nL350: \nL351: ###### Phase: 1 to 5\nL352: Total Units: 170 units\nL353: Completed Year: 2008\nL354: Sales Status: Sold out\nL355: \nL356: [Button: × ]\nL357: \nL358: ## cite48†Image L359: \nL360: ### The Ritz – 2 Storey Shop Office\nL361: ###### Total Units: 60 units\nL362: Completed Year: 2006\nL363: Sales Status: Sold out\nL364: \nL365: [Button: × ]\nL366: \nL367: ## cite49†Image L368: \nL369: ### Jalil Sutera 2 ½ Storey SuperLink House Phase 6\nL370: \nL371: ###### Total Units: 23 units\nL372: Completed Year: 2009\nL373: Sales Status: Sold out\nL374: \nL375: [Button: × ]\nL376: \nL377: ## cite50†Image L378: \nL379: ### Jalil Sutera 2½ Storey Semi-D Phase 7A\nL380: \nL381: ###### Total Units: 12 units\nL382: Completed Year: 2010\nL383: Phase: Phase 7A\nL384: Sales Status: Sold Out\nL385: \nL386: [Button: × ]\nL387: \nL388: ## cite51†Image L389: \nL390: ### Jalil Sutera 2½ Storey Semi-D Phase 7B (Twin)\nL391: ###### Total Units: 20 units\nL392: Completed Year: 2014\nL393: Phase: Phase 7B\nL394: Sales Status: Sold out\nL395: \nL396: [Button: × ]\nL397: \nL398: ## cite52†Image L399: \nL400: ### Aurora Serviced Suites\nL401: \nL402: #### @ Bukit Jalil\nL403: \nL404: GALLERY\nL405: \nL406: [Button: × ]\nL407: \nL408: ## cite53†Image L409: \nL410: ### Aurora Sovo\nL411: \nL412: #### @ Bukit Jalil\nL413: ###### The “Smart Office, Versatile Office” or SOVO comprises of 209 units of flexible office suites for both life-style or work. Sited above the 3-storey shop-offices of the Aurora Place, these spacious units allow for diversified opportunities to the discerning investors. Readily accessible to the hybrid mall, the entire complex shall extol high security features and great views of the surroundings.\nL414: \nL415: cite54†GALLERY L416: \nL417: [Button: × ]\nL418: \nL419: ## cite55†Image L420: \nL421: ### Kem Askar-Johore\nL422: ###### On 19 July 2012, the Company was awarded the contract for the construction of an integrated army complex in Johor (“KemAskar Project”) with a provisional contract value of RM87.00 million and is expected to be completed by first quarter of 2015, or over a 30-month period.\nL423: \nL424: cite56†GALLERY L425: \nL426: [Button: × ]\nL427: \nL428: ## cite57†Image L429: \nL430: ### Iraq – AL-Zohour Water Treatment\nL431: ###### On 9 August 2012, the Company was awarded a contract for the design and execution of civil and electro-mechanical, supply and installation of equipment and pipes, operating and maintenance of works for a water treatment plant in Baghdad, Iraq (“Al-Zohour Project”). The contract sum for this Project is approximately RM258.88 million (equivalent to Iraqi Dinar 100.34 billion).\nL432: \nL433: cite58†GALLERY L434: \nL435: [Button: × ]\nL436: \nL437: ## cite59†Image L438: \nL439: ### Iraq – Holy Karbala Road\nL440: ###### On 23 January 2013, the Company was awarded a contract for preparing, developing and maintaining Baghdad-Holy Karbala Road “Second Stage” from Musayyib/Jurf Al-Sakhar Crossroad to the entrance of Karbala City (“Karbala Project”). The contract sum for this Project is approximately RM61 million (equivalent to Iraqi Dinar 23.64 billion).\nL441: \nL442: cite60†GALLERY L443: \nL444: [Button: × ]\nL445: \nL446: ## cite61†Image L447: \nL448: ### Iraq – Al Hawraa City\nL449: ###### On 19 December 2013, the Company was awarded a contract for rehabilitation of 44 km length of the Al-hawraa District Roads and Rehabilitation for all the infrastructure and utilities services in Al-hawraa District, Iraq (“Al-Kut Project”). The Contract sum for this Project is Iraqi Dinar 71,392,461,600 or approximately USD58 million. The Contract was awarded by the Commission of Construction, Wassit Province in Iraq and terms confirmed on 20 February 2014.\nL450: \nL451: cite62†GALLERY L452: \nL453: [Button: × ]\nL454: \nL455: ## cite63†Image L456: ### Madagascar\nL457: \nL458: ###### This page is under construction\nL459: \nL460: SPECIFICATION\nL461: \nL462: cite64†WEBSITE L463: \nL464: [Button: × ]\nL465: \nL466: ## cite65†Image L467: \nL468: ### India\nL469: \nL470: ###### Rehabilitation and Upgrading of National Highway No.5 of India\nL471: \nL472: SPECIFICATION\nL473: \nL474: cite66†WEBSITE L475: \nL476: [Button: × ]\nL477: \nL478: ## cite59†Image L479: \nL480: ### Iraq\nL481: \nL482: ###### Preparing, Developing And Maintaining Baghdad\nL483: \nL484: SPECIFICATION\nL485: \nL486: cite67†WEBSITE L487: \nL488: [Button: × ]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLatest Publication - NAPIC (https://napic.jpph.gov.my/en/latest-publication)\nciteturn266view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://napic.jpph.gov.my/en/latest-publication\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 126\nL0: cite0†FAQ |  cite1†Contact Us |   cite2†Sitemap L1: \nL2: cite3†Login†prismlive.jpph.gov.my |  cite4†Portal Complaint |  cite5†PRISM 2.0 Complaint L3: # Latest Publication\nL4: No  | Document Name⇵  | Updated Date⇵\nL5: --- | --- | ---\nL6: 1  | cite6†A Comparative Analysis of Machine Learning Models for the Klang Valley Shop Price Index L7:  | 2026\nL8: 2  | cite7†NAPIC The Nation’s Definitive Source of Property Market Knowledge and Strategic Insights L9:  | 20